\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-2pt}

KL distillation replaces per-neuron BCE with output-distribution coupling: a predictor ($<$1\% of base parameters) halves TEAL's perplexity across 7--14B.
The predictor is self-sufficient; a 67.2M-parameter compensation network yields identical metrics at both 30\% and 50\% sparsity.
Perplexity alone is an unreliable deployment metric: at 30\%, uniform allocation delivers $7.5\times$ smaller cross-seed downstream $\sigma$ than global top-$k$, a requirement for reliable deployment.
At 14B, the PPL advantage persists but an MMLU gap emerges, consistent with scale-dependent decoupling of output and task-specific preservation.
Future directions include N:M structured sparsity for hardware-native acceleration and a single predictor serving multiple sparsity targets.

